4 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410522 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410544 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410523 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410544 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410524 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410544 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410526 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410544 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410530 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410544 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410544 1077 "selection" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410544 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410530 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410544 1079 #uuid "f77cbe95-6bf7-5129-86d2-ca625369224e" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410542 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410545 1069 "q-159782" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410545 1071 "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w})=2$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=2$" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410545 1072 #uuid "6a087057-8a12-50fd-9bce-b13921276b0f" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410545 1074 "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w})=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3$ (not $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410544 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410546 1069 "q-79311" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410546 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]})=$" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410546 1072 #uuid "a1e15f70-9949-53c2-8d3b-1dd646d018c1" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410546 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]})=3.$" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410541 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410547 1069 "q-35272" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410547 1071 "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]})=$" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410547 1072 #uuid "a83939de-1a77-573b-b113-0b677761ad51" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410547 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]})=2.$" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410543 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410548 1069 "q-70556" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410548 1071 "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410548 1072 #uuid "f5153a05-4fb4-55bc-add5-e73b5a837403" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true] [17592186410548 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$" 13194139533596 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-09726ccb-ffa4-5f18-9b63-38b552326b64" 17592186410521 "answer-143b59b0-133f-55a9-aba4-577093e492df" 17592186410522 "answer-47588aca-363b-5cbe-b2d3-66854ec8696e" 17592186410523 "answer-4f6e1323-5cbb-5d05-81d7-ed37a2420f70" 17592186410524 "answer-74a173e3-4894-57a1-b114-4cebc35d441c" 17592186410525 "answer-76ef7f6d-ef95-5749-93fb-20eeddd0b606" 17592186410526 "answer-8404cbe2-ec28-5347-a546-978b108f6afc" 17592186410527 "answer-84c4b891-3a0a-57b7-8450-2deb8c9ca291" 17592186410528 "answer-95748133-fe39-5ce6-8d35-f5c78273de56" 17592186410529 "answer-a84f8fed-839c-579a-aec7-743be12c797b" 17592186410530 "answer-ae69705d-4393-52b2-abc9-696b673d6331" 17592186410531 "answer-afbe1ce8-7c2f-59bd-b55a-918cd14fecdd" 17592186410532 "answer-b850f1a8-97df-56f1-9296-e435261baa73" 17592186410533 "answer-b88d0552-0930-5b71-a537-c45316717b44" 17592186410534 "answer-dba05347-6a57-5e05-bc58-97062bc9c25c" 17592186410535 "answer-e419addd-03bc-5503-b8f2-4323b5a419e1" 17592